  "query_preview": "..High pressure remains the dominant driver for our \nweather through Friday, despite being shunted a bit southwestward. \nThe movement of the ridge is due to a weak s/wv currently sitting \nover northern Nevada. This wave moves into southern Utah today and \nthen down the Arizona/New Mexico border Wednesday and Thursday.\n\nPersistent easterly through southeasterly winds the last several \ndays have allowed the Monsoonal moisture pool to move back northwest \ninto Arizona. This moisture, combined with minor cooling aloft, may \nallow isolated cells to develop over the next few days, but mainly \non Wednesday. We are not expected any impactful weather from these \nshowers/thunderstorms.\n\nThe ridge begins to break down quickly over the weekend, with \nsouthwesterly winds developing. A major trough looks to form over \nthe interior Pacific Northwest during this time frame and then dive \nsouthward into early next week.\n\nOnce this is established, there exists the potential to bring \nsignificant amounts of tropical moisture northward into Arizona. \nPerhaps even most of the remnants of Hurricane Rachel, as well as \nanother tropical circulation just outside of this forecast period. \nConfidence is growing quickly for this solution, as most models have \nnow converged upon it. This may be a major event for Arizona, but it \nis a little early to blow the whistle. Pay attention to the evolving \nforecast over the next few days.",
